 "answer": "I can't give you one reliable number. The live sources I found disagree, but most of them put Kuala Lumpur in the **Unhealthy** range (US AQI roughly 150–200).\n\n| Source | US AQI | Rating |\n|---|---|---|\n| IQAir | 207 | Very Unhealthy |\n| DailyAQI (OpenAQ data) | 185 | Unhealthy |\n| AQICN | 173 | Unhealthy |\n| AQI.in | 158 | Unhealthy (PM2.5 about 70 µg/m³) |\n| Time.now | 59 | Moderate |\n| Weather Underground | 97 | Moderate |\n\nThe numbers differ because each site uses different monitoring stations and updates at different times. Some pages also seemed to show cached or forecast values. The IQAir figure was from midday on Oct 16, so it's probably the oldest. The two \"Moderate\" results look like outliers.\n\n**What this means:**\n- Air quality in Kuala Lumpur is likely poor right now, mainly from PM2.5 (fine particles).\n- At these levels, sensitive groups (children, older adults, and people with asthma or heart or lung conditions) should limit time outdoors. Everyone else should cut back on prolonged or heavy outdoor exertion.\n- Consider wearing an N95 or KN95 mask outdoors, keeping windows closed, and running an air purifier indoors if you have one.\n\nFor the most authoritative reading, check the Malaysian Department of Environment's API (Air Pollutant Index) at https://eqms.doe.gov.my/. Note that it uses Malaysia's own API scale, not the US AQI. You can also check IQAir or AQICN for live updates.",
